# Nothing reads a .env file at the repo root. Settings live in two places; docs/keys-and-secrets.md lists
# every key, what it can do, and how to replace it.
#
# 1. The three apps. Copy each app's own example to .env.local in the same folder (git ignores it):
#      prototype/staff-app/.env.example
#      prototype/guest-portal/.env.example
#      prototype/checkin-app/.env.example
#    Only the project URL and the public anon key go there. The build refuses a service_role or secret key.
#
# 2. The Edge Functions. Supabase's secret store, never a file on a laptop. From prototype/:
#      npx supabase secrets set NAME=value
#    RESEND_API_KEY        send-emails: sends approved emails. Not set: they wait in the queue.
#    EMAIL_FROM            send-emails: the sender, e.g. "AAFE Gala <gala@your-verified-domain>"
#    GUEST_PORTAL_URL      send-emails: the guest portal's address, for sponsor links
#    GIVELIVELY_ORG_ID     givelively-sync: AAFE's Give Lively account
#    GIVELIVELY_API_KEY    givelively-sync: the Zapier API key. Not set: the sync returns not_configured.
#    CRON_SECRET           lets the scheduled job (database/cron.sql) call the sync and email functions
#    Demo only, remove for real AAFE use (docs/BACKEND.md):
#    GIVELIVELY_BASE_URL   points the sync at the givelively-mock function
#    EMAIL_REDIRECT_TO     sends every email to one inbox, with the real recipient in the subject
#    Testing emails on your own laptop with your own Resend key: npm run email:local (docs/BACKEND.md); it writes
#    prototype/supabase/functions/.env, which git ignores.
#    Supabase sets SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_ANON_KEY and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY itself.
